body:
`kalide init-library <path>` creates a new, deck-ready template library at `<path>`. It writes the library root: `library.yaml` (name derived from the target directory's base name and validated against the `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*` convention — an invalid name is a hard error, used verbatim and never sanitised; `format: 1` required; optional `description`), the standard top-level layout entries `slides/`, `sections/` and `media/` created empty, and a minimal `themes/default/theme.css` using only system fonts and neutral colours (mirroring the no-arg `kalide init` seed's `default` theme). "Empty library" means no templates: it has no slide/section templates and no media, and it DOES carry the minimal `default` theme so that a deck's theme — optional, defaulting to `default` (theme-selection) — resolves and the created library can immediately host a deck. The seed is exactly that: one `default` theme, no starter template, no media, no additional themes. The created library loads cleanly (templates-dir-validation). It also writes the library/template-author agent guide at the library root as a kalide-owned `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` full guide (library-agent-guide, agent-guide-reference) verbatim from a static file embedded in internal/scaffold, plus a library-root `AGENTS.md` pointer carrying the line `Kalide Reference: ./AGENTS_KALIDE.md`; the library layout permits these inert non-template root entries (library-guide-layout), so the created library still loads cleanly. `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` is written only when absent (an existing one is kalide-owned and left for `kalide upgrade`, upgrade-refresh-owned-scaffold); `AGENTS.md` is created carrying the reference when absent, left byte-for-byte untouched when it already contains the string, and otherwise appended to with the reference line while the author content is byte-preserved. The guide files are text-only, not template assets and inert to the library loader. The command is local-filesystem only: no network, no git (consistent with external-template-library, global.constraint.anonymous-install).

Argument and refusal rules: `<path>` is required — omitting it is a usage error (usage to stderr, non-zero exit, nothing written). It refuses to clobber (clear message, non-zero exit, nothing written; no `--force`): if `<path>` exists and already holds a `library.yaml`, or any of the standard layout entries `slides/`, `sections/`, `themes/` or `media/`, it fails. A pre-existing `AGENTS.md` at `<path>` is not one of these blocking entries: it never blocks init-library and is left byte-for-byte untouched (or gains the appended reference line when it lacks it). A non-existent `<path>` is created; an existing directory that is empty — or holds only unrelated files such as `.git`, a README, or an `AGENTS.md` — is allowed, and its unrelated files are left untouched (mirroring cli-init-refuse-existing). `<path>` existing as a non-directory is an error.

AC:
- `kalide init-library ../my-lib` in a fresh location creates `../my-lib/library.yaml` (name `my-lib`, `format: 1`), empty `slides/`, `sections/` and `media/`, `themes/default/theme.css`, `AGENTS_KALIDE.md`, and a referencing `AGENTS.md`; the result loads cleanly (templates-dir-validation);
- a deck whose `kalide.yaml` points `templates: ../my-lib` with no `theme:` resolves `default` and validates/serves (theme-selection), so `init-library` composes with `kalide init <path>`;
- a subsequent `kalide init-library ../my-lib` refuses (non-zero exit, nothing written);
- `kalide init-library dir` where `dir` already holds a `library.yaml` or any of the standard layout entries refuses and writes nothing;
- a target whose base name does not match `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*` (e.g. `My Lib`) fails with a clear error and writes nothing;
- `kalide init-library` with no `<path>` is a usage error and writes nothing;
- scaffolding into a library directory that already holds an author `AGENTS.md` succeeds: it is left byte-for-byte unchanged when it already carries the reference string, or gains the appended reference line with its remainder preserved when it does not, and `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` is written only when absent (agent-guide-reference).
